This file answers ONE question: is zig-libs, AS A WHOLE, still plain MIT? §1
answers it and states the rule that keeps the answer true; §0 says which
relationships to third-party work are capable of changing it. It is NOT an
index of the collection's provenance, and since 2026-09-06 it is not an index
of the modules that carry their own attribution either — both live at the
module that has them (§1, §2). A module's internal development origin — which
of the authors' own projects it was first prototyped in — is not recorded
anywhere: it carries no attribution weight and would read backwards once those
projects import zig-libs.

====================================================================
0. POLICY — what each relationship to third-party work requires
====================================================================

What creates an obligation is third-party source or third-party data travelling
with this code — not the act of implementing a published specification from its
prose. Accordingly:

  - A module that is pure clean-room-from-a-public-spec/RFC, with NO
    third-party source code ported and NO third-party implementation studied as
    a design reference, needs NO file of its own. Its RFC/spec citation lives
    in the module's own SPEC.md instead — see e.g. whois (RFC 3912), rdap
    (RFC 7480-family), tar (POSIX ustar + documented GNU extensions).
  - Third-party source actually ported/translated, or third-party DATA
    reproduced, requires its license terms reproduced — in
    `modules/<name>/NOTICE`, beside the files, so the notice travels with what
    owes it. Never in this file, and this file does not enumerate those modules
    either; §1 says how they are found instead.
  - A DESIGN REFERENCE — a named third-party implementation consulted for
    behavior, algorithm shape, or API design, with no source copied — is
    recorded for provenance so any future line-level reuse is caught, WITH ITS
    UPSTREAM LICENSE NAME. These are records, not conditions, and they live at
    the module (§2), never here: a repository-level file that also catalogues
    84 modules' design references stops being readable as the answer to "what
    do I owe?", which is the only question it exists to answer.
  - Exercising an installed third-party binary purely as a black-box
    compatibility test oracle (e.g. running `tar`/`nft` and diffing output
    against this module's own encoder/decoder) is neither of the above and
    needs no entry — no source or design was consulted, only the program's
    observable behavior against its own documented format. This covers
    CAPTURING that output once and committing it as a golden fixture, not only
    invoking the tool live: `tar`'s `write_golden_*.tar` are GNU tar's bytes
    wrapped around a `hello.txt` we wrote, and freezing them so the test needs
    no subprocess changes nothing about who authored what. The clause was
    written for live invocation, and this sentence exists because two
    independent readings in the 2026-08-12 audit found it did not literally
    reach the frozen case.

    THE LINE THIS DOES NOT CROSS. The exemption holds when the captured
    artifact is OUR content in the tool's format. It stops the moment the
    artifact also carries content the tool itself authored — a stock template,
    a default stylesheet, generated boilerplate — since that is no longer
    merely "the program's observable behavior" on our input. `zipstream`'s
    `xlsx_sample.xlsx` is exactly that case and is why the module carries its
    own provenance note: most of its bytes are LibreOffice's default theme and
    style tables, not ours. It equally stops at data that was never observed
    from a running program at all — numbers read out of an upstream source file
    by a script are reproduced data, not oracle output; `rescue` is that case.
    When in doubt, open the artifact; a fixture's description of itself is a
    claim, not a verification.

====================================================================
1. IS THE WHOLE LIBRARY STILL PLAIN MIT?
====================================================================

YES. zig-libs is distributed under the MIT License and carries no
redistribution condition of its own beyond it. Nothing in this file adds one.

**Code or data whose own licence is copyleft — GPL, LGPL, AGPL, or anything
else that would attach its terms to the distribution as a whole — is a DEFECT,
not a paperwork item.** It is removed, not documented. There is no entry in
this file, and no wording anywhere else, that makes such material acceptable:
the remedy is to delete it and re-implement what it did. That is the whole of
what this section is for. If the answer above ever becomes "no", the answer is
not amended here — the cause is taken out of the tree.

WHY THERE IS NO LONGER A LIST HERE (policy change, 2026-09-06). This section
used to enumerate the modules that carry their own `modules/<name>/NOTICE` —
twenty-six paths, maintained by hand. That enumeration is gone. A module that
ports third-party material has a legal question of its OWN, and it is
discharged where the material is, by the module's own NOTICE; restating which
modules those are made this file an index of somebody else's obligations
rather than an answer about the library, and made every new attribution file a
two-file edit whose second half was silently forgettable. This file is now
reserved for the one case that would oblige a change to the WHOLE library —
and per the rule above, that case is a defect to be removed, not an entry to
be added.

HOW TO FIND A MODULE'S OBLIGATION, now that nothing here points at it. Every
`modules/<name>/NOTICE` declares its kind on its own FIRST LINE:

    <name> — third-party attribution      carries a CONDITION
    <name> — provenance note              record only, no condition

so the condition-bearing files are enumerated by reading first lines rather
than by trusting an index — `head -1 modules/*/NOTICE` is the entire query,
and `zig build check-catalog` holds every such file's first line to exactly
one of those two shapes, which is what makes the query sound. Every condition
so far is the same one: keep the notice with the files. If you redistribute a
module, ship its NOTICE with it.

WHAT ENFORCES THE RULE ABOVE. `zig build check-copyleft` reads each shipped
file's OWN licence declaration — an `SPDX-License-Identifier:` naming a
copyleft licence, or an FSF grant paragraph — rather than searching for the
string "GPL", because naming a copyleft licence is not the defect and being
under one is. Two things in this tree say "GPL" and neither is a violation:
`modules/ebpf/src/testdata/*.bpf.c` set `_license = "GPL"`, which is a value
the kernel's BPF verifier reads to decide which helpers a program may call and
not a copyright notice (`modules/ebpf/README.md` explains it), and
`modules/poseidon` names LGPL-3.0 circomlib as a comparison ORACLE it runs
against, shipping none of it.

====================================================================
2. DESIGN REFERENCES — recorded at the module, not here
====================================================================

Design references live at the module that has them: in
`modules/<name>/README.md`'s `Provenance:` statement — the default, and the
only provenance location `zig build check-catalog` reads — or, when the record
needs more than a paragraph or makes a licensing argument that must not be
compressed, in a `modules/<name>/NOTICE` provenance note. They moved out of
this file on 2026-08-14, where they had run to ~1000 lines indexing 84 modules.
`modules/<name>/SPEC.md` is deliberately NOT a destination: it is the
verification-methodology file, and it is where "studied as a design reference"
most easily drifts into "verified against".

None of those records imposes any obligation beyond zig-libs' own MIT license,
even though the projects they name include GPL-2.0, GPL-3.0, LGPL-2.1,
LGPL-3.0 and AGPL-3.0 ones — a design reference carries no license condition at
all, which is why §1's defect rule is about what is SHIPPED and not about what
is named. A module with no such record needs none: pure clean-room from a
public spec/RFC, per §0. A full provenance/license audit was completed
2026-07-09 and re-examined 2026-07-29; both are recorded in the project git
history.
